      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\nx - \\frac{1}{x} = 4\n\\]\nWe aim to find \\( x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} \\). First, we will find \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\). We square both sides of the given equation:\n\\[\n\\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = 4^2\n\\]\nExpanding the left side, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16\n\\]\nSimplifying the middle term:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16\n\\]\nRearranging to solve for \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\):\n\\[\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16 + 2 = 18\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity for \\( x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} \\):\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\left( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) - \\left( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)\n\\]\nWe already know \\( x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4 \\) and from above, \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18 \\). We need \\( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\). Squaring \\( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\):\n\\[\n\\left( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18 + 2 = 20\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nx + \\frac{1}{x} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting back into the expression for \\( x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} \\):\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 4 \\left( 18 \\right) - 2\\sqrt{5} = 72 - 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{72 - 2\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]",
