      "text": "Method 1 (Factorization Formula): \nWe start by using the factorization formula to simplify the expression.\n$$\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\left(x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\left( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) - \\frac{1}{x^3}\n$$\nTo find $x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}$, we can square the original equation and subtract 2:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 &= x^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\\\\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} - 2 &= 4^2 \\\\\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} &= 16 + 2 \\\\\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the factorization formula:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} &= 4 \\left(18 - 1\\right) \\\\\n&= 4 \\times 17 \\\\\n&= \\boxed{68}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMethod 2 (Cubing the Original Equation): \nWe consider both $x$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ as a single variable, which we denote as 'a'. Thus, we have:\n$$\na - \\frac{1}{a} = 4\n$$\nWe aim to find $a^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3}$. Cubing both sides of the equation yields:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left( a - \\frac{1}{a} \\right)^3 &= a^3 - 3a - \\frac{3}{a}- \\frac{1}{a^3} \\\\\n4^3 &= a^3 - 3a - \\frac{3}{a}- \\frac{1}{a^3} \\\\\n64 &= a^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3} - 3 \\left(a + \\frac{1}{a} \\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a + \\frac{1}{a} = 4$, so:\n$$\n64 = a^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3} - 3 \\left(4 \\right) \\\\\n64 = a^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3} - 12 \\\\\na^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3} - 76 &= 0 \\\\\na^3 - \\frac{1}{a^3} &= \\boxed{76}\n$$",
